Article 6

1. The forms of Violence referred to in Article 5 include:a. physical violence;
b. psychological violence;
c. sexual violence;
d. economic violence;
e. exploitation; andf. KBGO.

16 - (2) Economic Violence as referred to in paragraph (1) letter d includes:
a. taking full control over the victim's income, including salary, savings, or other financial assistance received by the victim:b. restricting or prohibiting the victim from owning or using money, so that the victim is completely dependent on the perpetrator for financial needs:c. prohibiting or preventing the victim from working or continuing education with the aim of limiting the victim's financial independence:d. using the victim's assets or property for personal gain or damaging such assets as a form of punishment; ande. making all financial decisions without involving the victim, so that the victim has no control over household finances.
3. Exploitation as referred to in paragraph (1) letter e at a minimum:a. exploiting women's labor by providing inadequate wages, imposing excessive working hours, or not providing safe and healthy working conditions;
b. exploiting women for sexual purposes, by coercion, deception, or pressure. Including human trafficking for the purpose of prostitution or pornography production; andc. exploiting women unfairly in economic transactions, such as providing loans with very high interest or manipulating the market for personal gain.
4. KBGO as referred to in paragraph (1) letter f, includes:a. comments or messages that are insulting, demeaning, or belittling women;

17 -
b. messages or comments that threaten to commit physical or sexual violence against women;
c. disseminating the victim's personal information (such as address, telephone number, or other identity) without permission with the aim of intimidating or harming the victim;
d. extortion involving threats to spread intimate images or videos of the victim unless the victim meets the perpetrator's demands;
e. spreading private photos or videos of an intimate nature without the consent of the woman in the content;
f. use of technology to stalk or monitor the victim continuously, often with the aim of frightening or controlling the victim; andg. digitally altering images or videos of women for the purpose of insulting, humiliating, or sexually exploiting.

Article 9

1. Prevention of acts of violence against women as referred to in Article 8, is carried out by Regional Apparatus that administer governmental affairs in the fields of:
a. health;
b. education;
c. manpower;
d. cooperatives, small and medium enterprises and industry;
e. social affairs;
f. communication and informatics;
g. culture;
h. tourism;
i. environment;
j. transportation;
k. trade;
l. youth and sports;
m. politics;
n. public tranquility, order, and community protection;
o. public works and spatial planning;
p. public housing and residential areas;
q. women's empowerment and child protection;
r. population administration and civil registration;
s. population control and family planning; and
t. other related fields.
2. Prevention of acts of violence by Regional Apparatus as referred to in paragraph (1), is carried out in an integrated and continuous manner based on the Regional Action Plan coordinated by the Regional Apparatus that administers governmental affairs in the field of Women's Protection and Empowerment.

22 -

Article 7

The implementation of the Protection of Women includes efforts to:
a. prevent and protect women from acts of violence, exploitation, and discrimination in a rapid, integrated, and coordinated manner;
b. provide services to victims of violence, exploitation and discrimination;
c. empower women;
d. fulfill the special needs of women; and e. improve the quality of families.

18 -

Article 11

1. Forms of services for the protection of women and women victims of violence include:
a. receiving reports and outreach to Victims;
b. providing information about Victims' Rights;
c. facilitating the provision of health services;
d. facilitating the provision of psychological strengthening services;
e. facilitating the provision of psychosocial services, social rehabilitation, and social reintegration;
f. providing legal services;
g. identifying economic empowerment needs;
h. identifying temporary shelter needs for Victims and Victims' Families that need to be immediately fulfilled;
i. facilitating the needs of Victims with Disabilities;
j. coordinating and cooperating on the fulfillment of Victims' Rights with other Institutions; and
k. monitoring the fulfillment of Victims' Rights by law enforcement apparatus during the judicial process.

24 -

(2) The implementation of forms of services for the protection of women and women victims of violence as referred to in paragraph (1) is in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.

Article 5

1. Every woman victim of Violence, Exploitation and Discrimination has:
a. the right to handling;
b. the right of Victims to Protection; and
c. the right of Victims to recovery.
2. The right to handling as referred to in paragraph (1) letter a includes:
a. the right to information on all processes and results of Handling, Protection, and Recovery;
b. the right to obtain handling result documents;
c. the right to legal services;
d. the right to psychological strengthening;
e. the right to health services including examination, treatment, and medical care;
f. the right to services and facilities in accordance with the special needs of victims; and
g. the right to deletion of sexual content for cases of sexual violence using electronic media.

15 -

3. The right of Victims to Protection as referred to in paragraph (1) letter b includes:
a. provision of information regarding rights and Protection facilities;
b. provision of access to information on the implementation of Protection;
c. protection from threats or violence by perpetrators and other parties as well as recurrence of violence;
d. protection of identity confidentiality;
e. protection from attitudes and behavior of law enforcement officials that demean Victims;
f. protection from loss of employment, job transfer, Education, or political access; and
g. protection of Victims and/or reporters from criminal prosecution or civil lawsuits for Crimes of Sexual Violence that have been reported.
4. The right of Victims to recovery as referred to in paragraph (1) letter c includes:
a. medical rehabilitation;
b. mental and social rehabilitation;
c. social empowerment;
d. restitution and/or compensation;
e. social reintegration; and
f. obtaining skills training for economic improvement for vulnerable women.

Article 3

1. The implementation of women's protection is intended to realize the protection of women from acts of violence, exploitation and discrimination.
2. The implementation of women's protection aims to:
a. prevent all forms of violence, exploitation, and discrimination against women;
b. provide services and recovery for women victims;
c. fulfill the special needs of women; and
d. improve the quality of life of women.
3. The scope of regulation of the implementation of women's protection includes:
a. women's rights;
b. women's protection efforts;
c. obligations and responsibilities of the Regional Government;
d. institutions;
e. coordination and cooperation;
f. participation;
g. monitoring, evaluation and reporting;
h. awards; and
i. financing.

Part Two

Prevention of Violence, Exploitation and Discrimination ArticIe 8 (1) The Regional Government carries out efforts to prevent violence, exploitation and discrimination against women in an integrated manner in the Region.

2. The prevention of violence, exploitation and discrimination against women is intended to create a safe environment for women and to reduce the vulnerability to violence, exploitation and discrimination of vulnerable women groups.
3. Vulnerable women groups as referred to in paragraph (2) include:a. women in disaster and social conflict situations;
b. women with disabilities; c. women with HIV/AIDS;
d. women migrant workers;
e. women heads of households;
f. women domestic workers;
g. women home-based workers;
h. elderly women;
i. women informal workers;
j. women in situations of intolerance; and k. other vulnerable women groups.
4. Prevention efforts as referred to in paragraph (1) and paragraph (2) are implemented by:a. conducting coordination, integration, synchronization of the prevention of acts of violence, exploitation and discrimination based on partnership patterns;
b. conducting socialization regarding laws and regulations related to the protection of women's rights;
c. building and providing a complete and easily accessible information system for the prevention of violence, exploitation and discrimination;

19 -
d. providing easy access to obtain civil rights, education, health, employment, training and funding, welfare improvement and services;
e. developing media for communication, information, education and public campaigns through media; f. developing educational materials and curricula;
g. developing safe transportation systems and public spaces;
h. building early detection systems, security and integrated complaint services in industrial areas, companies, residential areas, educational environments, Islamic boarding schools and other public spaces;
i. providing consultation services for families, prospective brides and grooms, students, Islamic students, youth, university students and vulnerable groups;
j. encouraging behavioral change of parents and the community;
k. developing policies related to regencies/cities, sub-districts, schools, Islamic boarding schools, and universities that are safe and friendly for women and integrated with regional spatial planning policies;
l. forming and developing cadres, communities, and groups from among youth, students, Islamic students, university students, journalists, social media influencers, educators, and religious leaders for prevention;
m. conducting education and advocacy to owners, managers and/or users of social media; n. developing protection systems and special support for vulnerable women groups;
o. conducting awareness-raising for perpetrators;

20 -
p. increasing the capacity of Regional Apparatus, government agencies, educational institutions, religious institutions, health service provider institutions, professional institutions, business sector, service institutions, Islamic boarding schools, youth organizations, legal aid organizations, media, religious leaders, community leaders and communities; andq. forming cooperation networks in prevention efforts from acts of violence, exploitation and discrimination with law enforcement apparatus, government agencies, Regional Apparatus, community institutions, educational institutions, community leaders, religious institutions, health service provider institutions, professional institutions, business sector, service institutions, Islamic boarding schools, youth organizations, legal aid organizations, communities, and media.
5. Efforts to prevent Violence, Exploitation and Discrimination against women in an integrated manner as referred to in paragraph (1) are coordinated by the Regional Apparatus that administers governmental affairs in the field of women's empowerment and child protection as well as governmental affairs in the field of population control and family planning.
6. Prevention efforts as referred to in paragraph (3) letter a may involve Regional Apparatus, agencies, community institutions, educational institutions, community leaders, integrated service posts, family learning centers, religious institutions, health service provider institutions, professional institutions, business sector, service institutions, Islamic boarding schools, youth organizations, legal aid organizations, communities, and media.

21 -

Article 12

1. Empowerment of Women victims of acts of violence, exploitation and discrimination is carried out in an integrated manner in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.
2. Empowerment of Women victims of acts of violence, exploitation and discrimination as referred to in paragraph (1) is directed to obtain opportunities and rights as human beings to be able to play a role and participate in activities:
a. health;
b. education;
c. economy;
d. law;
e. manpower;
f. politics;
g. government;
h. social affairs;
i. culture; and
j. environment.

CHAPTER VII

PARTICIPATION

Article 30

1. Every person may participate in the implementation of the Protection of Women in the Region. (2) Participation as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be carried out by individuals, groups as well as - 34 - community social organizations, non-governmental organizations, educational institutions, Law Enforcement Institutions, service institutions, religious institutions, Islamic boarding schools, professional organizations, legal aid organizations, youth organizations, broadcasting supervisory institutions, media, communities, professional institutions, the business sector and other related stakeholders.
3. Participation as referred to in paragraph (1) may take the form of:a. providing information or reporting to the competent authority if aware of acts of violence, and or exploitation and or discrimination against women;
b. taking preventive action against violence against women;
c. conducting professional skills training in the form of:
1. leadership training;
2. management training;
3. business communication training;
4. negotiation skills training;
5. technology and digital training; and
6. entrepreneurship training;
d. conducting specific skills training in the form of:
1. self-defense training;
2. creative arts skills training; and
3. handicrafts, cooking skills, sewing skills, childcare skills, and household management skills;
e. conducting health and welfare training in the form of:
1. fitness training; and
2. mental health training;
f. conducting community and social training in the form of: - 35 -
1. activism and advocacy training; and
2. volunteer training in various social, health, and environmental fields;
g. establishing community social institutions that handle the development and empowerment of women.
4. Further provisions regarding community participation as referred to in paragraph (2) shall be regulated in Mayor Regulation.

CHAPTER V

INSTITUTIONAL ARRANGEMENTS

Article 26

1. The Regional Government in its efforts to provide and implement protection services for women victims of violence, exploitation and acts of violence shall establish UPTD PPA.
2. UPTD PPA as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be under and responsible to the head of the Regional Apparatus implementing governmental affairs in the field of women's empowerment and child protection.
3. UPTD PPA as referred to in paragraph (2) shall have the duties of:
a. receiving reports or outreach to Victims;
b. providing information about Victims' rights;
c. facilitating the provision of health services;
d. facilitating the provision of psychological strengthening services;
e. facilitating the provision of psychosocial services, social rehabilitation, social empowerment, and social reintegration;
f. providing legal services;
g. identifying economic empowerment needs;
h. identifying temporary shelter needs for Victims and Victims' Families that need to be fulfilled immediately;
i. facilitating the needs of Victims with Disabilities;
j. coordinating and working together on the fulfillment of Victims' rights with other institutions; and - 32 -
k. monitoring the fulfillment of Victims' rights by law enforcement apparatus during the judicial process.
4. UPTD PPA as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be grouped into the following roles:
a. health;
b. psychology;
c. social-education;
d. law; and
e. economy.
5. Further provisions regarding the establishment, main duties and functions as well as management of UPTD PPA as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be regulated by Mayor Regulation.

