a. health;
b. psychology;
c. social-education;
d. law; and
e. economy.
5. Further provisions regarding the establishment, main duties and functions as well as management of UPTD PPA as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be regulated by Mayor Regulation.

Article 7

The implementation of the Protection of Women includes efforts to:
a. prevent and protect women from acts of violence, exploitation, and discrimination in a rapid, integrated, and coordinated manner;
b. provide services to victims of violence, exploitation and discrimination;
c. empower women;
d. fulfill the special needs of women; and e. improve the quality of families.

18 -

Article 5

1. Every woman victim of Violence, Exploitation and Discrimination has:
a. the right to handling;
b. the right of Victims to Protection; and
c. the right of Victims to recovery.
2. The right to handling as referred to in paragraph (1) letter a includes:
a. the right to information on all processes and results of Handling, Protection, and Recovery;
b. the right to obtain handling result documents;
c. the right to legal services;
d. the right to psychological strengthening;
e. the right to health services including examination, treatment, and medical care;
f. the right to services and facilities in accordance with the special needs of victims; and
g. the right to deletion of sexual content for cases of sexual violence using electronic media.

15 -

3. The right of Victims to Protection as referred to in paragraph (1) letter b includes:
a. provision of information regarding rights and Protection facilities;
b. provision of access to information on the implementation of Protection;
c. protection from threats or violence by perpetrators and other parties as well as recurrence of violence;
d. protection of identity confidentiality;
e. protection from attitudes and behavior of law enforcement officials that demean Victims;
f. protection from loss of employment, job transfer, Education, or political access; and
g. protection of Victims and/or reporters from criminal prosecution or civil lawsuits for Crimes of Sexual Violence that have been reported.
4. The right of Victims to recovery as referred to in paragraph (1) letter c includes:
a. medical rehabilitation;
b. mental and social rehabilitation;
c. social empowerment;
d. restitution and/or compensation;
e. social reintegration; and
f. obtaining skills training for economic improvement for vulnerable women.

Article 3

1. The implementation of women's protection is intended to realize the protection of women from acts of violence, exploitation and discrimination.
2. The implementation of women's protection aims to:
a. prevent all forms of violence, exploitation, and discrimination against women;
b. provide services and recovery for women victims;
c. fulfill the special needs of women; and
d. improve the quality of life of women.
3. The scope of regulation of the implementation of women's protection includes:
a. women's rights;
b. women's protection efforts;
c. obligations and responsibilities of the Regional Government;
d. institutions;
e. coordination and cooperation;
f. participation;
g. monitoring, evaluation and reporting;
h. awards; and
i. financing.

Article 4

1. Every woman has the right:
a. to life;

11 -

b. to form a family and continue offspring;
c. to self-development;
d. to obtain justice;
e. to personal freedom;
f. to security;
g. to welfare; and
h. to participate in government.
2. The right to life as referred to in paragraph (1) letter a includes:
a. the right to maintain life and improve her standard of living;
b. the right to obtain peace of mind, security, peace, happiness, physical and spiritual welfare; and
c. the right to a good and healthy environment.
3. The right to form a family and continue offspring as referred to in paragraph (1) letter b, namely the right to form a family and continue offspring through lawful marriage in accordance with religion and the provisions of laws and regulations.
4. The right to self-development as referred to in paragraph (1) letter c, includes:
a. the right to the fulfillment of her basic needs to grow and develop properly;
b. the right to protection for the development of her personality, to obtain education, educate herself and improve the quality of life to become a faithful, pious, responsible, noble, happy, and prosperous human being in accordance with human rights;
c. the right to develop and obtain benefits from science and technology, arts and culture in accordance with human dignity for the welfare of herself, the nation, and humanity;

12 -

d. the right to communicate and obtain information necessary to develop her personality and social environment in accordance with religious, social, and legal norms;
e. the right to seek, obtain, own, store, process, and convey information using all types of available means in accordance with religious, social, and legal norms;
f. the right to fight for the right to self-development, both individually and collectively, to build society, nation, and state in accordance with religious, social, and legal norms;
g. the right to carry out social work in accordance with religious, social, and legal norms;
h. the right to participate in the planning and implementation of government policies in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations;
i. the right to organize and form organizations in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations; and
j. the right to organize education and teaching in accordance with religious, social, and legal norms.
5. The right to obtain justice as referred to in paragraph (1) letter d, includes:
a. the right to receive equal treatment without discrimination;
b. the right to obtain justice;
c. the right to submit applications, complaints, and lawsuits, whether in criminal, civil, or administrative cases; and
d. the right to be tried through a free and impartial judicial process, in accordance with procedural law that guarantees an objective examination - 13 - by an honest and fair judge to obtain a just and correct decision.
6. The right to personal freedom as referred to in paragraph (1) letter e, includes:
a. the right not to be enslaved or subjugated, trafficked, and all acts with similar purposes;
b. the right to personal integrity, both spiritual and physical;
c. the right not to be the object of research without her consent; and
d. the right to freely express and convey opinions with due regard to religious values, morality, order, public interest, and national integrity.
7. The right to security as referred to in paragraph (1) letter f, includes:
a. the right to protection of her person, family, honor, dignity, and property rights;
b. the right to recognition before the law as a human person; and
c. the right to be free from threats to do or not do something that violates religious, social, and legal norms.
8. The right to welfare as referred to in paragraph (1) letter g, includes:
a. the right to have welfare, either alone or together; and
b. the right to perform work and receive fair wages in accordance with her work that is comparable and commensurate with human dignity in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.
9. The right to participate in government as referred to in paragraph (1) letter h, includes:
a. the right to be elected and to vote in general elections; regional head elections, and/or - 14 - elections for other political positions based on equal rights through direct, general, free, secret, honest and fair voting according to applicable laws and regulations;
b. the right to pursue a career in government; and
c. the right to convey aspirations directly or through intermediaries in government.

